<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marienberg Fortress</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Festung Marienberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marienberg Fortress stands on a hill above the Main opposite the old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The Marienkirche on the summit dates from the early eighth century, and from the thirteenth century to 1719 the fortress was the residence of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Würzburg. It was stormed by the Swedes in 1631 and later rebuilt as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> fortress; it now houses museums.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marienberg-fortress.jpg" alt="Marienberg Fortress" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Horvat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marienberg_wuerzburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The fortress occupies a spur of the 266-metre Marienberg, which rises about 100 metres above the left bank of the Main, with vineyards on its slopes. Around 1000 BCE people of the Urnfield culture built a refuge castle here, and finds suggest that under the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>, around 500 BCE, the hill had trade contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancient-greece">Ancient Greece</a> and may have been the seat of a prince. A 7th-century document calls the fortification Uburzi, the origin of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>; the name Marienberg, Mary's Mount, came from the chapel built under Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heden-ii">Hedan II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From around 1200 the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> fortified the hill, and Bishop Hermann von Lobdeburg moved his court there permanently in 1253 after quarrels with the townspeople, who sided with the Emperor. In 1373 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> attacked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> and the garrison answered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons, the first recorded use of guns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>. In 1525, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, a peasant army of 15,000 besieged it without breaching the walls; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> then outflanked the besiegers and more than 8,000 peasants were killed. The sculptor Tilman Riemenschneider was imprisoned and tortured there that year.</p><p>Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-echter-von-mespelbrunn">Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn</a> completed its transformation into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> residence. Swedish troops plundered it in 1631 and carried most of its library to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. It became a Reichsfestung in 1648 and its outer works took their present form in 1711 to 1715. Once the court had moved across the river, it served only as a military post, changing hands in the wars of 1795 to 1815, and lost its status as a fortress in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Incendiary bombs dropped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> on 16 March 1945 burned much of the fortress, and rebuilding lasted from 1950 to 1990. The Museum für Franken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> armoury shows Franconian art including Gothic sculpture by Riemenschneider, and the Fürstenbaumuseum tells 1,200 years of the history of the city and the fortress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marienberg Fortress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its wealth began with the silver of the Erzgebirge, mined at Freiberg since the twelfth century and, after new strikes from the 1470s, at Schneeberg, Annaberg and <b>Marienberg</b>; the large silver coins struck there from about 1500 were among the first of the type later called the thaler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Heden II supported the Anglo-Saxon missionary Willibrord, who received estates at Arnstadt in 704, and a church was founded on the <b>Marienberg</b> at Würzburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>The prince-bishop was elected by the noble cathedral chapter and governed from the <b>Marienberg</b> fortress, later from the town below.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
